  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Carnivore Diet</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">An all-animal-food elimination diet with strong short-term anecdotes but essentially no long-term clinical evidence.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/diets/">← Back to Diets</a>
  </header>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is the Carnivore Diet?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The carnivore diet eliminates every plant food and eats only animal products — meat, fish, eggs, and
        for some followers a limited amount of dairy. Some stricter versions (sometimes called the "lion
        diet") narrow further to just meat, salt, and water. There's no single official rulebook or governing
        organization behind it, so exact versions vary from one follower to the next.
        <br /><br />
        Most people who try it describe a health motivation rather than a performance or environmental one: in
        the largest self-reported survey of carnivore dieters to date, 93% cited health reasons for starting.
        The appeal is largely framed as an elimination diet taken to its logical extreme — remove essentially
        everything except meat, and any symptom that improves must have been caused by something you cut out.
        It's also credited anecdotally with relief from autoimmune and IBS-type digestive symptoms, though
        that's self-reported and anecdotal rather than something clinical trials have confirmed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        There's no macro target or calorie structure the way there is with keto or flexible dieting — the
        entire "rule" is the exclusion list. Fruits, vegetables, grains, legumes, nuts, seeds, and added sugar
        are all removed, leaving meat (often including organ meats like liver), fish, eggs, and sometimes
        butter, cheese, or other dairy. Because virtually all carbohydrate is removed along with the produce,
        most followers end up in ketosis by default, similar to keto, but without keto's more moderate protein
        target or any plant-food allowance at all.
        <br /><br />
        Because the diet removes entire food groups rather than following a specific macro or calorie formula,
        there's also no standard "compliant vs. non-compliant" list the way Whole30 has — individual followers
        decide for themselves whether dairy, honey, or certain spices count as acceptable exceptions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Evidence</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>There are no randomized controlled trials of the carnivore diet.</strong> A 2026 scoping
        review in <em>Nutrients</em> — the most comprehensive evidence assessment done on the diet so far —
        identified only nine human studies published between 2021 and 2025, and every one of them was
        observational: five case studies, two social-media surveys, one modeling study, and one exploratory
        study, with no random assignment and no control group in any of them. The review rated the overall
        evidence quality as NHMRC Levels III–IV — the lowest tiers on that evidence hierarchy — and concluded
        that "it is not currently possible to reliably assess the long-term safety" of the diet, adding that
        long-term adherence "cannot be recommended" given how little is actually known.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6643/18/2/348" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the scoping review →</a>
        <br /><br />
        The largest single study is a self-reported social-media survey of 2,029 adults who said they'd
        followed a carnivore diet for at least six months (median 14 months). Respondents reported high
        satisfaction and improvement across a range of self-assessed conditions, and among the subset who
        reported lab values, average LDL cholesterol was markedly elevated (172 mg/dL) while HDL (68 mg/dL)
        and triglycerides (68 mg/dL) looked favorable — a mixed lipid pattern sometimes seen on very-low-carb,
        high-fat diets more broadly. But this is self-reported data from a self-selected group of committed
        carnivore dieters recruited through social media, with no independent verification of the lab
        values — a limitation other researchers have specifically challenged in a published response, and one
        the original authors partially conceded while defending the plausibility of the numbers.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34934897/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the survey →</a>
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9154227/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the published critique of its methodology →</a>
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Independent assessment:</strong> Harvard T.H. Chan's Walter Willett has called the diet
        "basically a terrible idea," and Cleveland Clinic's dietitian-reviewed overview lists heart disease,
        colorectal cancer risk from high red-meat intake, and nutrient deficiencies among its concerns. Neither
        source found evidence supporting the diet's health claims beyond short-term weight loss from cutting
        sugar and refined carbs.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://hsph.harvard.edu/news/carnivore-diet-terrible-idea/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read Harvard T.H. Chan's assessment →</a>
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://health.clevelandclinic.org/the-carnivore-diet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read Cleveland Clinic's overview →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Nutritional Concerns / Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Zero dietary fiber.</strong> Removing every plant food removes 100% of dietary fiber, which
        has well-established evidence behind it for cardiovascular and metabolic health — see our <a href="/supplements/fiber/">fiber page</a> for the research on soluble/insoluble fiber's role in
        cholesterol, blood sugar, and gut health. The 2026 scoping review found carnivore dieters' fiber intake
        typically fell below 1g per day against a 25–30g recommended adequate intake — essentially a complete
        elimination, not just a reduction.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Micronutrient gaps.</strong> The same review flagged deficiency risk across thiamine, vitamin
        C, vitamin D, magnesium, calcium, iron, iodine, and folate — nutrients concentrated in the fruits,
        vegetables, and grains the diet removes entirely. Vitamin C and folate are worth flagging specifically
        since they have essentially no significant carnivore-compatible sources at all beyond small amounts in
        organ meats.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Documented scurvy risk.</strong> This isn't hypothetical: a published case report describes a
        man who developed clinically confirmed scurvy on two separate occasions roughly two years apart after
        eating an almost entirely meat-based diet, going through extensive misdiagnosis both times before
        vitamin C deficiency was recognized as the cause.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10916690/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the case report →</a>
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Cardiovascular risk from saturated fat.</strong> The diet's near-total reliance on red meat and
        animal fat pushes saturated fat intake well past the WHO's recommended upper limit, and the scoping
        review reported elevated total and LDL cholesterol across the available studies — a real,
        mechanistically-understood cardiovascular risk factor. The picture isn't uniformly bad: some
        self-reported carnivore dieters show a "mixed" lipid pattern with high LDL alongside favorable HDL and
        triglycerides, and proponents point to that pattern as reassuring. But that data comes from
        unverified self-report in a self-selected group, not from trials measuring actual cardiovascular
        outcomes, so the long-term risk from sustained high LDL on this diet remains genuinely unresolved
        either way.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Other flagged risks.</strong> The scoping review and independent reviews also note increased
        colorectal cancer risk associated with high red-meat intake generally, kidney stress and kidney stone
        risk from very high animal protein intake (documented in at least one case in the review), and
        digestive issues like constipation from the complete absence of fiber.
        <br /><br />
        People with kidney disease, a history of kidney stones, or existing high LDL cholesterol should be
        especially cautious, as should vegetarians and vegans for obvious reasons. Anyone considering it long
        rather than as a short, medically-supervised elimination experiment should know that essentially no
        research exists on what happens beyond a year or two of adherence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

